  "body": "Found by reading, in the second read of PR #3502. It is not a fault of that PR: the code is on `main`. **Nothing was run. This is a hypothesis.** The first step is a test that proves or disproves it.\n\n## The hypothesis\nThe hole recognizers `recognize` and `recognize_cross` (`crates/motion/src/drilling/recognize.rs:87,97`) classify a hole from its wall facets with `WALL_NZ 0.7` and a circle fit with `FIT_TOL 0.02` (`recognize.rs:39,44`), in the setup frame.\n\nA hole whose axis is tilted 2 degrees from the tool axis has a section that differs from a true circle by about 0.0006 r. That is far below the fit tolerance. Such a hole can pass as a Z hole. The drilling operation then drills along Z, and the drill cuts into the wall of the real hole. This is a gouge that passes with no message.\n\n## Why P1\nIf the hypothesis is true, the posted program cuts in the wrong place and nothing blocks the post (the table: \"a gouge or collision that passes\").\n\n## Wanted\n1. A test with a part that has one hole tilted 2 degrees and one tilted 5 degrees from the tool axis (made from code, no model file). Assert what detection gives for each: a Z hole, a skipped hole with a name, or a cross hole.\n2. If a tilted hole is taken as a Z hole: a tolerance on the angle of the axis (small, for example the angle at which the drill moves the wall by more than the tolerance of the operation over the hole depth), and a named skip for a hole outside it. Record the tolerance as a decision. Consider a parameter with a default (CAM choices are user parameters).\n3. If the test shows that detection already refuses or skips the tilted hole: keep the test as a pin and close this issue with the commit.\n",
